course: dictionary
lesson: 5812
title: Magnesium: a metal and a mineral
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Magnesium is one word with two meanings. In a science lesson it is a light, silvery-white metal that burns with a very bright white flame. In a health lesson it is a mineral your body needs, found in green vegetables, nuts and whole grains. The context tells you which meaning is meant.
explain: «Trick birthday candles have a metal powder, magnesium, in the wick that catches fire again from the glowing wick.» The words metal powder tell you magnesium is a metal, not a food. The words catches fire and glowing tell you it burns. Try the swap: "a metal powder, green vegetables" does not fit, so the metal meaning is right.
explain: Magnesium is used in fireworks, flares and light metal parts for bikes and cars. When it is mixed with other metals it makes a light alloy. Words like muscles, nerves and «rich in magnesium» tell you the health meaning.
fact: Magnesium is a chemical element with the symbol Mg and the number 12.
fact: Magnesium is a light, silvery-white metal.
fact: Burning magnesium ribbon gives a dazzling white light, so you must never look straight at it.
fact: Burning magnesium leaves white ash called «magnesium oxide».
fact: Your body needs magnesium to help muscles and nerves work well, and you get it from green vegetables, nuts and whole grains.
exercise: «Trick birthday candles have a metal powder, magnesium, in the wick.» What kind of thing is magnesium here? | A vegetable | A colour | A type of nut | *A metal
exercise: Ella reads «Eat green vegetables and nuts, because they are rich in magnesium.» What does magnesium mean here? | A type of candle | A kind of bike part | *A mineral your body needs | A bright white flame
exercise: What colour is the metal magnesium? | *Silvery-white | Bright red | Green | Black
test: Which food is a good source of magnesium? | *Whole grains | Sweets | Ice cream | Fizzy drinks
test: When magnesium ribbon burns, what does it give off? | A loud bang like a drum | *A very bright white light | Blue fog | Smoke that smells of sweets
exam: Which phrase in this lesson names the white ash left after magnesium burns? | Magnesium ribbon | Metal powder | Rich in magnesium | *Magnesium oxide
